---
/**
 * Cisco Cybersecurity case study overlay (Figma node 402:4079).
 *
 * The long-form variant: prose sections with supporting assets, a row of
 * prototype screens, and a closing banner out to the full deck. Opened by the
 * `rust` carousel card.
 *
 * Header, copy, highlights, and hero come from the registry entry so this
 * study reads the same on the card and in the overlay; only the body blocks
 * live here. The design's byline reads "Boardable | Summer 2025"; the registry
 * keeps the carousel's "DesignMap | 2022".
 *
 * Mock-ups whose copy is still lorem ipsum in the deck are cropped to leave it
 * out (the quick-view drawer and the prototype's Detect tab) or left out (the
 * notes tab).
 */
import CaseStudyOverlay from "../CaseStudyOverlay.astro";
import ProseBlock from "../case-study/ProseBlock.astro";
import AssetRow from "../case-study/AssetRow.astro";
import Banner from "../case-study/Banner.astro";
import { ciscoCybersecurity, toOverlayProps } from "../../lib/case-studies";

import deckCover from "../../assets/case-studies/cisco-cybersecurity/deck-cover.png";
import designProcess from "../../assets/case-studies/cisco-cybersecurity/design-process.png";
import securexRibbon from "../../assets/case-studies/cisco-cybersecurity/securex-ribbon.png";
import legacyWorkflow from "../../assets/case-studies/cisco-cybersecurity/legacy-workflow.png";
import journeyMap from "../../assets/case-studies/cisco-cybersecurity/journey-map.png";
import respondToIncident from "../../assets/case-studies/cisco-cybersecurity/respond-to-incident.png";
import prototypeIncidentList from "../../assets/case-studies/cisco-cybersecurity/prototype-incident-list.png";
import prototypeDetect from "../../assets/case-studies/cisco-cybersecurity/prototype-detect.png";
import prototypeInvestigate from "../../assets/case-studies/cisco-cybersecurity/prototype-investigate.png";
import prototypeResponseActions from "../../assets/case-studies/cisco-cybersecurity/prototype-response-actions.png";
import quickViewDrawer from "../../assets/case-studies/cisco-cybersecurity/quick-view-drawer.png";
import riskScore from "../../assets/case-studies/cisco-cybersecurity/risk-score.png";
import incidentTabs from "../../assets/case-studies/cisco-cybersecurity/incident-tabs.png";
import responseActions from "../../assets/case-studies/cisco-cybersecurity/response-actions.png";
import incidentsCasesModel from "../../assets/case-studies/cisco-cybersecurity/incidents-cases-model.png";
---

<CaseStudyOverlay {...toOverlayProps(ciscoCybersecurity)}>
  <ProseBlock
    heading="Background"
    layout="stacked"
    image={designProcess}
    imageAlt="A three-diamond design process: explore and define, ideate and decide, then design and deliver"
    imageCaption="DesignMap's triple-diamond design process."
    fit="contain"
  >
    <p>
      This project took place in 2022, as part of my work as a UX Lead at
      DesignMap. Our client was the Cisco Security division. DesignMap teams had
      previously worked on smaller features and design systems, but this project
      was much larger in scope. The client wanted to completely revamp their
      SecureX cybersecurity suite ahead of the RSA conference the following
      April.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock heading="My role">
    <p>
      I joined as a UX Lead, DesignMap’s level above Senior Designer, on a team
      of five: two senior product designers, a part-time information architect,
      a senior engagement lead, and me. The engagement lead and information
      architect formally led the project, while we three designers owned the UX
      research, design, and production work.
    </p>
    <p>
      As the most senior designer, I led through influence rather than
      reporting lines. I helped the other two designers make sense of the
      process, tying deliverables like journey maps back to the bigger picture
      so the team could keep moving forward.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="A house of brands"
    image={securexRibbon}
    imageAlt="The legacy Secure Endpoint dashboard, with the SecureX ribbon open over it listing separate applications"
    imageCaption="The SecureX ribbon, linking separate products over a dashboard of charts."
  >
    <p>
      Like many complex enterprise software suites, the initial offering grew
      out of a series of acquisitions. Stakeholders lamented a history of
      “shipping the org chart,” with many acquired products cobbled together.
      The platform had become a “house of brands”: separate tools with
      inconsistent and distracting color schemes, logos, and stylized names, and
      no shared workflow.
    </p>
    <p>
      The products were arbitrarily separated feature sets, presented as
      “applications” with vague names and no real integration of workflows.
      There was no coherent mental model for what each product was or what it
      did. Cisco had tried to unify them with a floating UI element known as
      “the ribbon,” but it did little more than link between products and
      provide a data clipboard. Meanwhile, the dashboard was a wall of charts
      that did little to direct attention.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="A convoluted workflow"
    layout="stacked"
    image={legacyWorkflow}
    imageAlt="A winding path through the 18-plus steps of the legacy incident response workflow, from logging into SecureX to determining a course of action"
    imageCaption="Responding to one incident took 18 or more steps, across three or more tabs."
    fit="contain"
  >
    <p>
      To respond to a single incident, an analyst logged into SecureX and
      clicked the Incidents tab, which opened in a new browser tab. From an
      incident, they launched an investigation in yet another tab, reviewed the
      relation graph, saved the investigation as a “snapshot,” and linked it
      back to the incident. Then they copied the list of observables, opened the
      ribbon to create a case in the casebook, pasted the observables in, linked
      the case to the incident, and added notes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Only then could they determine a course of action, and each action meant
      going to the relevant point product or acting manually. In all, it took
      three or more tabs, three separate objects, 18 or more steps, and a lot of
      copying and pasting.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock heading="Primary objectives">
    <p>
      Our mission was to re-envision a loosely connected platform as a new, more
      focused product. We also had two specific goals. The first was to reduce
      “mean time to remediation” (MTTR): could an analyst viewing an incident
      notification investigate, formulate a response, and take action in less
      time? The second was to enable junior analysts to perform at a higher
      level: could a junior analyst respond to incidents that would otherwise
      require a more senior analyst?
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Journey mapping"
    image={journeyMap}
    imageAlt="The Project EVO journey map, with stages, tasks, pain points, and opportunities for novice and expert analysts"
    imageCaption="The journey map for novice and expert analysts. It later set the incident’s four tabs."
  >
    <p>
      The team seemed to be struggling with the relevance of journey mapping.
      They felt uncertain about how it should relate to the conceptual work the
      information architect was doing in parallel. I encouraged them to think of
      journey maps as a model of users’ needs and process, and to use the
      exercise to highlight pain points and opportunities.
    </p>
    <p>
      That reframing got the team unstuck. We focused on the high-priority
      scenarios that users typically undertook, and the journey maps went on to
      shape the rest of the project, including the final four-tab incident
      structure: Detection, Investigation, Response actions, and Notes.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Focusing on the primary flow"
    layout="stacked"
    image={respondToIncident}
    imageAlt="The “Respond to incident” stage of the journey map, covering a novice analyst’s awareness, validation, isolation, investigation, and escalation steps"
    imageCaption="The “respond to incident” stage the first prototype was designed around."
  >
    <p>
      To focus on MTTR, we designed our first prototype around triage and
      incident response, which is also the primary task of junior analysts. We
      asked how analysts might notice a high-priority incident, how they might
      determine what is happening and what they can do, and how they might take
      action.
    </p>
    <p>
      We then ran a concepting workshop with stakeholders. We reviewed the
      research so far, ideated and sketched concepts, and voted. The workshop
      let us align closely with the stakeholders’ vision, but also glean
      implicit ideas and insights.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Initial prototype"
    image={prototypeIncidentList}
    imageAlt="The prototype’s incident list, with risk scores, sources, assignees, and recommendations"
    imageCaption="The first prototype’s incident list, testing whether the table’s information was useful."
  >
    <p>
      Based on insights from the workshop, we designed a simple prototype with
      four sections. A single incident list screen tested comprehension of the
      left navigation and the value of the information in the table. The
      “Detect” tab, across four screens, tested whether participants could
      understand an incident from its timeline and overview, probed on data
      density, and looked at filtering and the ability to drill down.
    </p>
    <p>
      The “Investigate” tab, across three screens, tested whether participants
      could grasp an incident from a relationship map, and whether it was
      intuitive to interact with. For the fourth section, a “Response Actions”
      tab, we asked participants what they would want to see there.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <AssetRow
    assets={[
      {
        image: prototypeDetect,
        alt: "The prototype’s Detect tab, with an incident’s event timeline",
        caption: "Detect: an incident’s event timeline.",
      },
      {
        image: prototypeInvestigate,
        alt: "The prototype’s Investigate tab, with a relationship map of observables",
        caption: "Investigate: a relationship map of the observables.",
      },
      {
        image: prototypeResponseActions,
        alt: "The prototype’s Response actions tab, with recommended actions and bulk actions",
        caption:
          "Response actions: recommendations, and a way to run them in bulk.",
      },
    ]}
  />

  <ProseBlock heading="Concept testing results">
    <p>
      Participants were excited about the more streamlined experience, but we
      also learned some important nuances. They found the dashboard distracting
      and unnecessary. They distrusted severity ratings based on dollar amounts.
      And they kept asking for the “what,” which showed a need to surface key
      data points more quickly.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="A quick-view drawer"
    layout="stacked"
    image={quickViewDrawer}
    imageAlt="The incidents list with the quick-view drawer open, showing the incident’s details, recommended actions, and targets"
    imageCaption="A quick-view drawer, so an analyst can read an incident without leaving the list."
  >
    <p>
      A note on attribution for the refined screens: visual elements such as
      color, fonts, and icons came mostly from previous DesignMap projects or
      from a teammate who focused on visual design. The page layout, information
      hierarchy, flow, and most other elements were my work.
    </p>
    <p>
      With a side drawer pattern, we could surface top-priority information in
      the incident table and critical secondary information in the drawer. The
      drawer was also a convenient way to support our primary user, the junior
      analyst, with summaries and links to reference material such as the
      industry-standard MITRE ATT&amp;CK framework, without leaving the page.
      This gave junior analysts a way to progressively learn in context.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Severity score"
    image={riskScore}
    imageAlt="A risk score breakdown: 930, from a 93% probability of financial loss times an asset value index of 10"
    imageCaption="The revised risk score: probability of loss, times an asset value index."
  >
    <p>
      After we shared the finding that participants did not trust a
      dollar-impact score, we worked with a stakeholder and subject matter
      expert who had been working on a different system of prioritization. In
      the new score, risk was part of a compound score that still expressed
      financial loss but was more accessible to analysts. Participants in the
      second round of testing found it much more useful.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Workflow-driven tabs"
    layout="stacked"
    image={incidentTabs}
    imageAlt="An excerpt of the journey map above an incident header with Detection, Investigation, Response actions, and Notes tabs"
    imageCaption="Four tabs, mapped from the journey: Detection, Investigation, Response actions, and Notes."
  >
    <p>
      Within an incident, we created a simple tab structure to organize the
      tasks involved in understanding and responding to it. The first three
      tabs, Detection, Investigation, and Response actions, followed naturally
      from the workflow and the products we were trying to integrate. Our
      detailed journey map and discussions with participants helped us identify
      an opportunity for a fourth: Notes, a space for documentation and
      collaboration. With proper journey mapping, 18 steps and a myriad of
      applications came down to four tabs.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Detailed mock-ups"
    image={responseActions}
    imageAlt="The response actions tab of an incident, with recommended actions and workflows, bulk actions, and actions taken"
    imageCaption="The response actions tab, combining recommendations with what had already been done."
  >
    <p>
      The detailed mock-ups covered four screens. The incidents list displayed
      key information to help analysts understand impact, status, and potential
      actions, and its details drawer offered recommended actions to enable
      junior analysts and speed up remediation. Within an incident, the response
      actions tab combined recommendations with history, centralizing action
      planning and making collaboration with other analysts more transparent.
      The notes tab documented work, enabling collaboration as well as learning:
      junior analysts could get help, and also learn from old incidents.
    </p>
    <p>
      In the second round of testing, participants responded to the product
      vision: “It brings the functionality of a lot of the tools we use into one
      place.” Of the information architecture, one said, “Everything was where I
      expected it.” And of the incident response flow: “I wish I had something
      like this today. It helps get a picture of what happened.”
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Incidents and cases"
    layout="stacked"
    image={incidentsCasesModel}
    imageAlt="A model of how incidents and cases relate to investigations, response actions, notes, tracking, and data"
    imageCaption="Incidents and cases, modeled as related objects with different starting points."
  >
    <p>
      Late in the project, stakeholders began asking about “cases,” a
      pre-existing concept in the current product. Their purpose was not clear:
      they lived separately from incidents but could be linked to them, and they
      had not been identified as core to the junior analyst, so they had not
      been our focus. Stakeholders themselves were not always clear on the
      purpose of the different concepts and terms, and there was little time to
      track down information from busy Cisco team members. Separately, a new PM
      leader had been hired to lead the group, and we engaged her in several
      focused sessions. Her perspective was very illuminating.
    </p>
    <p>
      We noticed that, across different workflows and participants, these terms
      might reflect different starting points. In a top-down investigation, the
      system alerts you based on predefined patterns. In a bottom-up
      investigation, you initiate it yourself, for example based on intelligence
      community reports. Based on these modes of work, I modeled how incidents
      and cases could be different-but-related objects. With this model, we
      could see how the platform’s various experiences should work together.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock heading="Impact">
    <p>
      We delivered a prototype, principles, and documentation to help Cisco’s
      internal team continue the work. The clearest signal of our impact was the
      more focused user flow. Triaging and acting on an incident went from 18 or
      more steps across three or more separate tools to three: select the
      incident from the list, review the investigation tab, and run remediation
      from the actions tab.
    </p>
    <p>
      The work was featured in Cisco’s XDR launch at RSA 2023 (<a
        href="https://newsroom.cisco.com/c/r/newsroom/en/us/a/y2023/m04/cisco-unveils-new-solution-to-rapidly-detect-advanced-cyber-threats-and-automate-response.html"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">press release</a
      >). With the rest of the team, I co-presented the final readout to Jeetu
      Patel, then Cisco’s EVP of Security &amp; Collaboration, and the project
      was nominated for a UX Award.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cisco used this work to rebuild their platform, retired the legacy SecureX
      experience by the end of 2024, and continues to build on this foundation (<a
        href="https://www.cisco.com/site/us/en/products/security/xdr/index.html"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">Cisco XDR</a
      >). The new platform won more than 450 customers in its first 10 months
      after launch (<a
        href="https://blogs.cisco.com/security/xdr-means-so-much-more-than-some-may-realize"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">Cisco blog</a
      >).
    </p>
    <p>
      The project also helped drive future workshops and engagements for
      DesignMap, supporting the company at a critical time. It stands out as one
      of the most visible and impactful projects of my career.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <Banner
    variant="section"
    title="Full deck available"
    body="The original deck covers the work in more detail, including the journey maps, workshop artifacts, more of the prototype, and the straw-man screens I made to test the platform’s navigation."
    href="https://www.figma.com/deck/SIH4JZ1mk69GyhuungiqNT/-case-study--Cisco-XDR--2022-?node-id=0-1&t=p5f658bMHtKmeqv0-1"
    actionLabel="View the deck"
    image={deckCover}
    imageAlt="Title slide: Cisco XDR, Morgan Keys, DesignMap UX Lead, 2022, beside the incidents list with a malware incident open"
  />
</CaseStudyOverlay>
